---
title: "Go: compute the SHA-256 hash of os.Executable()"
---

This is just a quick code snippet that I want future me to remember.

<div><a href="https://goplay.tools/snippet/b-CeGbrkiO3">▶️ goplay.tools</a></div>

```go
package main

import (
	"crypto/sha256"
	"encoding/hex"
	"errors"
	"fmt"
	"hash"
	"io"
	"log"
	"os"
)

func ExecutableSum(h hash.Hash) (sum []byte, err error) {
	exe, err := os.Executable()
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}

	f, err := os.Open(exe)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}
	defer func() {
		err = errors.Join(err, f.Close())
	}()

	_, err = io.Copy(h, f)
	if err != nil {
		return nil, err
	}

	sum = h.Sum(nil)
	return sum, nil
}

func main() {
	exe, err := os.Executable()
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}

	sum, err := ExecutableSum(sha256.New())
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}

	fmt.Printf("The SHA-256 sum of %q is %q\n", exe, hex.EncodeToString(sum))
}
```

Quick comments for future me:

- Remember that `hex.EncodeToString()` can't error, so you can use it inline instead of extracting it into the surrounding block. That's nice.
- You probably will want to extract the `os.Open()` `f` handle into a `func` so that the `defer` runs ASAP. If you do it inside a larger function, then that `defer f.Close()` will happen only when you `return` which may be much later than you prefer.
- `os.Executable()` is not supported (it always errors) on `GOOS=wasip1`.
